# std/seq — indexed access. Implemented by tuck_rt.
# Bounds are a precondition: an out-of-range index is a program error
# reported at the call site, not a result the caller matches on.
extern:
  fn at[T]({items: Seq[T], index: int}) -> T
  fn setAt[T]({items: Seq[T], index: int, value: T}) -> void
  # `push` returns the grown seq rather than mutating `items` in place —
  # `at`/`setAt` mutate through a receiver the caller already owns as a
  # `var`, but growing can reallocate, so there is no shared backing store
  # to mutate through. Value semantics, same convention as std/random's
  # threaded state: `xs = {items: xs, value: v} push`.
  fn push[T]({items: Seq[T], value: T}) -> Seq[T]
  # `count`, not `len` — and that is the FIX, not a workaround. PROTOCOLS.md
  # makes `count(target)` the verb for "how many" across every collection, so
  # a Seq answering `count` is what the convention already said it should do.
  #
  # It also dodges the reason `len` could not be declared, which is STILL
  # LIVE — re-tested 2026-09-11, after the mangling fixes, in case those had
  # cleared it. They had not, because it is not a mangling problem: a runtime
  # `len(items: seq[T])` is ambiguous with `system.len` at every unqualified
  # `.len` inside tuck_rt.nim itself ("ambiguous call; both system.len and
  # tuck_rt.len match"). `.len` stays as the accessor sugar it always was.
  #
  # Worth knowing for anyone re-testing: compiling tuck_rt.nim ALONE passes.
  # The ambiguity sits in a generic body, and Nim does not check one until it
  # is instantiated — so the standalone compile is a false negative and only
  # a program that calls `count` shows it.
  fn count[T]({items: Seq[T]}) -> int
